WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.626
Ugly. Depressing.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.422
A cross between a giant square box 
and a federal penitentiary.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.841
These are all ways that 
people have described

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.050
this building behind me.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:12.429
It's Evans Hall at the University 
of California, Berkeley.

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:15.849
It's built in a style of architecture 
called brutalism.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:18.601
And when you look at college campuses 
around the US...

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:21.354
you'll see brutalist architecture 
like this everywhere.

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:24.399
Why are there so many brutalist 
buildings on campuses?

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:27.944
And what about this style of architecture
makes it so divisive?

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:33.908
Okay.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:36.077
Just to start with, 
the name Brutalism

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:38.079
doesn't actually have anything to do with

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.457
the brutality of the architecture per se.

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:43.084
Although some may argue that

00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:45.545
it’s quite harsh in appearance. 

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:48.757
It's actually derived from 
a French phrase.

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:49.716
Béton brut.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:52.510
The raw concrete 
in French.

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:54.012
This is Timothy Rohan.

00:00:54.512 --> 00:00:58.224
He's a professor of architectural history 
at UMass Amherst.

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:01.102
A campus known for its stunning,
brutalist architecture.

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:03.271
It's also the home of UMass Brut...

00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:06.066
a campus organization that raises awareness

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:07.942
of the school's collection of buildings.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.694
Some architects...

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:13.156
in conversations about Brutalism 
and its origins come up a lot.

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:17.994
One of them is the Swiss-born 
French architect Le Corbusier.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:21.539
He built an enormous building
in Marseilles, an apartment building.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:23.083
The Unité d’Habitation.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.377
This really became a model 
for the use of concrete...

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.839
and a model for what became brutalism.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:31.091
Concrete was widely used

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.802
but sometimes before this, 
you would maybe

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:36.179
sand the surface, 
paint the surface...

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:39.808
clean it up in a groundbreaking fashion.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.978
He left the concrete surface raw.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.481
Le Corbusier continued to build on the motifs

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:49.651
he used in Unité d’Habitation 
in his later work.

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:51.069
Other architects of the day

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:52.987
played in the Brutalist sandbox as well...

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.907
such as Marcel Breuer, 
Paul Rudolph...

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:59.327
Högna Sigurdardóttir
and Louis Kahn.

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:02.330
Brutalism became not just 
a style of architecture...`

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:04.874
but an entire esthetic ethos.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:09.087
In what became a manifesto of sorts 
for the movement...

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.798
architecture, writer, and critic 
Reyner Banham

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.675
decreed that new brutalist structures

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:15.385
should have the following qualities:

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:18.346
One: Memorability as an image.

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:21.432
Two: Clear exhibition of structure.

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:25.687
And three: Valuation of materials “as found.”

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.106
Modernist architecture up to this point

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:31.151
was dominated by people like 
Ludwig Mies van der Rohe...

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.237
who built sleek glass corporate skyscrapers...

00:02:34.362 --> 00:02:36.698
which featured what are called 
curtain walls:

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.408
Non load-bearing veneers

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:40.326
that were meant to hide the construction.

00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:43.329
Some architects say if you have 
a whole city like this

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.790
it's going to become 
banal, monotonous.

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:48.877
Modernist architects called 
these buildings fish bowls.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:52.630
And they were lampooned for their 
resemblance to graph paper.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.300
In contrast, brutalist buildings strove for

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.510
honesty in their materials 
and structure.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:00.180
They showed you how they were constructed.

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:02.307
At the end of World War II...

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:04.434
College attendance shot up.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.936
There was the veterans returning 
from the war

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.314
who were eligible for government benefits

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:11.399
that allowed them to go to college 
and pay for it.

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:15.069
This necessitated that universities 
build new facilities to handle

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.362
ballooning admissions.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.740
And with so many new buildings 
being needed...

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.950
what did architects of the day turn to?

00:03:21.618 --> 00:03:23.912
Brutalism.

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:25.914
Sorry.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:30.293
Schools like Yale had entire campuses
of brutalist architecture spring up

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.880
Which received glowing marquee reviews 
in the New York Times.

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:35.590
Campuses across the country

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.842
erected some pretty spectacular buildings...

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:41.638
that typified the challenging and idealistic, 
brutalist esthetic.

00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:45.808
Schools like Harvard, UMass Dartmouth, 
UC San Diego...

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:49.437
the University of Chicago, and
of course, Berkeley.

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:52.065
Some of the colleges like Yale University

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:53.942
they wanted to part from the old models

00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:55.485
of the Gothic and the Georgian.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:59.239
A campus that was like Oxford 
or Cambridge for elite gentlemen.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.866
Now they want to show that 
they are becoming

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:04.118
a world class research university.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:08.289
A university like mine, 
University of Massachusetts...

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.834
expanded at this time from 
an agricultural college...

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:14.754
and it becomes a research university.

00:04:15.046 --> 00:04:17.465
A flagship campus of Massachusetts.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:21.636
And so they start to become 
the hosts for modern architecture...

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:25.014
to show their progress.

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:29.018
During the 1970s, the US economy 
took a downturn.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.438
New construction on campuses 
ground to a virtual halt...

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.775
and maintenance of existing facilities 
was neglected...

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:39.195
leaving many buildings 
brutalist and otherwise...

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:41.656
dingy and in need of renovation.

00:04:41.656 --> 00:04:44.951
And there's real cutbacks 
in maintenance during the 70s.

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:46.327
It's noticeable.

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:47.745
And there's even a policy

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:50.790
that many campuses had 
called deferred maintenance.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.334
We're not going to do repairs.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:56.045
We're not going to unstop the gutter.

00:04:56.421 --> 00:04:59.299
We're not going to put in air conditioning 
that was never installed.

00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:01.968
It's deferred for a future date.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:04.012
When will this day come?

00:05:04.595 --> 00:05:05.972
I think it's maybe today.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.684
The 1980s swept in a new era
of construction

00:05:09.684 --> 00:05:11.644
that was a definite change of pace.

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:14.022
Architects like Robert Venturi designed

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:16.649
university buildings that featured 
brighter colors...

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:20.486
asymmetrical shapes and 
playful design elements.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:23.531
Instead of attempts to preserve or renovate

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:25.658
brutalist buildings in need of repair

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.785
numerous campuses across the country

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:30.955
opted to tear them down and 
build something shiny and new.

00:05:31.205 --> 00:05:32.623
And that is what it seems like

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:35.335
will be the fate for poor Evans Hall 
here in Berkeley.

00:05:35.626 --> 00:05:38.796
Although a campus spokesperson told me 
that there is, quote,

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:43.051
“No project in the works, or approved 
to demolish Evans Hall...”

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:44.135
They went on to say that

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:47.472
they're in the process of relocating 
all classrooms and offices

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:48.473
out of the building.

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:51.976
And will then construct new buildings 
to house them, at which point

00:05:51.976 --> 00:05:54.604
they will propose a project 
to demolish Evans.

00:05:54.812 --> 00:05:57.732
Which sounds to me like 
they're going to tear it down.

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:00.401
The reason for all of this 
is a study that showed

00:06:00.401 --> 00:06:03.446
that its seismic rating 
against earthquakes is poor.

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:07.075
So why is there no talk about 
tearing down this building?

00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:08.201
Or this one? 

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:09.660
Or this one? 

00:06:10.161 --> 00:06:13.206
These buildings and over 
22 others on campus...

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:15.875
share the same seismic rating as Evans.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.711
So why set your sights on just Evans?

00:06:18.961 --> 00:06:22.548
It's often about a number of other considerations, 
like real estate.

00:06:23.508 --> 00:06:26.177
The fact that these sites are 
valuable space

00:06:26.177 --> 00:06:28.179
on college campuses 
is at a premium.

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:31.057
The real reason may be buried
further in the report

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:33.935
which says that, quote, 
“Due to its massing height...

00:06:34.268 --> 00:06:37.230
scale and materiality, Evans Hall

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.108
which obstructs views 
to San Francisco and beyond...

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:41.692
is considered incongruous

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:44.195
with the Beaux Arts Buildings 
in the Classical Core.”

00:06:44.278 --> 00:06:46.447
And that, quote, “the ad-hoc placement”

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:48.783
of the new buildings “lacked sensitivity.”

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.493
My council is, be patient. 

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:54.872
Just because you find something 
unfashionable at the moment...

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.708
doesn't mean you should eradicate it.

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:00.044
But while Brutalism hasn't been seen

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:02.422
in a positive light for a few generations...

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.841
all hope is not necessarily lost.

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:07.718
There does seem to be a bit of 
a renaissance happening.

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:10.888
Dr. Rohan hopes to spread 
the gospel of Brutalism

00:07:10.888 --> 00:07:12.098
to a new generation

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:14.058
by doing tours of the brutalist buildings

00:07:14.058 --> 00:07:15.726
on the campus of his university.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.895
And Zillow has even named Brutalism

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:20.690
as one of the top trends to watch
in 2024.

00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:23.776
When you look around, 
Brutalism influence

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:26.737
has actually made its way into many corners of our culture.

00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:30.241
There are $1,000 concrete 
West Elm coffee tables...

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.494
and Jay-Z and Beyoncé paid 
$200 million

00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.663
for a brutalist inspired house 
on the ocean.

00:07:36.205 --> 00:07:38.332
I think it has seeped into popular culture...

00:07:38.499 --> 00:07:40.126
through Instagram, 
through Pinterest.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.002
All these things.

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:43.212
It is a very expressive architecture.

00:07:43.546 --> 00:07:47.049
It photographs really well; 
it's like cats on the Internet.

00:07:48.885 --> 00:07:50.094
It just spreads.

